//! The answers (spec §5.7, §8): the status word every syscall returns, the
//! reason a process was terminated, and the §8 process status.
//!
//! `SosStatus` is the one thing in this module a process must agree with the
//! kernel about beyond the calling convention, which is why it carries a
//! `describe` and conforms to `Printable` and `Error` where the op numbers
//! beside it carry nothing.
//!
//! Reached through the `sosabi` facade; see `lib.saw`.

// =============================================================================
// Status words (spec §5.7)
// =============================================================================

/// The status a syscall returns in the status register.
///
/// A status is for what the caller could not have known. A caller error the
/// process could have checked (an invalid handle, an op the object does not
/// have, a right it was not given, driving a thread that is not itself)
/// terminates the process and is not in this enum at all: it is a `FaultReason`
/// below. What is left here is the class a correct process can still meet:
/// resource exhaustion and peer death.
///
/// `BadHandle`, `BadOp` and `AccessDenied` live in `FaultReason` and keep their
/// numbers, so the tags read the same in a kernel report and cannot be confused
/// with a status a process might branch on. Values 1-3 are left as a gap here
/// for that reason: reusing them would make an old build's `BadHandle` and a
/// new build's status collide on the wire.
///
/// These tags are part of the contract in a way the op numbers are not, because
/// a process branches on them, so the backing is fixed-width (a value that
/// crosses an ABI pins its width) even though the register carrying it is
/// wider.
///
/// It is a status, not an error: an enum with an `Ok` case describes how a call
/// went, and only some of those ways are failures. The `Sos` prefix says whose
/// status it is; the hosted runtime has its own frozen `SysError` (errno tags,
/// `sawc/rt/ABI.md`) and the two contracts are unrelated.
///
/// `Ok` is a case because this enum is the wire table and 0 is in it. It is the
/// one value that is not a failure, and `sos`'s `checked` is the single place
/// that turns it into `Ok(())`, which is why an `Err(SosStatus.Ok)` cannot be
/// constructed anywhere downstream.
///
/// `Unknown` is not a status the kernel returns. It is where the userspace
/// mapping's `from(raw:)` miss lands, so a tag this build does not define stays
/// a typed error instead of being read as success. It is unreachable while both
/// halves compile from this one declaration, and exists for the build skew that
/// static linking makes impossible and a mapped vDSO will not.
///
/// `BadImage` (5) is a malformed process image handed to `process_create`.
/// Image bytes are data, on the `from(raw:)` precedent: an unrecognized wire
/// byte is data a reader rejects, never a trap, and a launcher that fetches
/// images dynamically must be able to report a bad download rather than die of
/// it. It is the one place the faults line falls on the status side for
/// something the caller supplied, because the caller did not write those bytes:
/// it was handed a region and asked to launch it.
///
/// `Drained` (6) is the boot-handle iterator having nothing left to give. It is
/// a status and not a fault because a loop that drains an iterator ends by
/// meeting it, so faulting would make the correct program the one that dies. It
/// stays drained: every later call answers the same.
///
/// `QuotaExceeded` (7) is policy's voice, and `NoResource` is the machine's. A
/// caller can act on the difference: `NoResource` says the slab is full (a
/// machine-wide edge, nothing this process can do about it), while
/// `QuotaExceeded` says this process has spent its budget, which a release of
/// something it already holds will fix. A launcher meeting the first has met
/// the end of the machine; a program meeting the second has met the end of its
/// allowance, and the two want different code. It is a status and not a fault
/// because quota headroom is dynamic resource vocabulary: it changes as objects
/// the process does not own are freed and as its peers release, so it is
/// precisely what a caller could not have checked.
///
/// `Revoked` (8) is the peer-gone doctrine's one word. It answers a call that
/// was parked on something which can no longer happen: for example, a
/// `Waiter.Wait` whose Waiter reached zero references while the caller was on
/// its blocked list. Nothing parked is ever silently doomed: every "what you
/// are waiting for is gone" is a delivered wake carrying a distinguishable
/// status, never a strand for the deadlock report to name later. It is a status
/// and not a fault because the mistake belongs to whoever released the handle,
/// and this word is delivered to somebody else, a sibling thread that made no
/// error. A parked caller is also in the one position from which nothing could
/// have been checked in advance. It is one word for every revocation site, so
/// it is not named for the Waiter. It covers the case where the object being
/// waited on stopped existing under the waiter.
///
/// `PeerClosed` (9) is the peer-gone doctrine's other word, §2.1's own term. It
/// answers a call whose far end no longer exists: a `Post` through an inlet
/// whose outlet column reached zero (nobody can ever take it), and a `Take`
/// through an outlet whose inlet column reached zero and whose ring has been
/// drained (no sender can ever appear, since attenuation is monotonic and the
/// count is zero).
///
/// `PeerClosed` and `Revoked` must not fold into each other. `Revoked` is the
/// object being waited on ceasing to exist under a parked caller: nothing to
/// act on, only to report. `PeerClosed` is information a protocol acts on: the
/// connection is still there and still yours, and what changed is that the
/// other role is unoccupied. A client meeting it stops sending; a server
/// meeting it closes the session.
///
/// `PeerClosed` is terminal. Nothing un-closes: the ends' reference columns
/// only ever fall to zero and a zero column can never rise (a handle onto an
/// end is what would raise it, and there is none), so a caller may test it as
/// often as it likes and will be told the same.
///
/// `WouldBlock` (10) is one transient for both directions: a `Post` whose ring
/// is full, and a `Take` whose ring is empty. It says call again, because the
/// answer will change when the peer acts, which is the opposite of `PeerClosed`
/// and why the two could not share a word. It is one case and not two because a
/// status is narrow when it names one condition, and post-full and take-empty
/// are the same condition: the ring is not in a state to serve this call yet.
/// Which direction it was is already in the op the caller made, so a
/// `PostWouldBlock` beside a `TakeWouldBlock` would encode the receiver twice
/// and force every poll loop to match two tags that mean one thing.
/// `describe()` is worded for the condition rather than for either op. It is a
/// status and not a fault because how full a shared ring is changes as the peer
/// acts, so a caller could not have checked it in advance. It is `NoResource`'s
/// neighbour in spirit, a resource edge, and differs in being the peer's to
/// clear rather than the machine's.
public enum SosStatus: UInt8 {
    case Ok = 0,
    case NoResource = 4,
    case BadImage = 5,
    case Drained = 6,
    case QuotaExceeded = 7,
    case Revoked = 8,
    case PeerClosed = 9,
    case WouldBlock = 10,
    case Unknown = 255,
}

extension SosStatus {
    /// A short human-readable tag, for a diagnostic or a log line.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case Ok -> "ok",
            case NoResource -> "out of kernel objects",
            case BadImage -> "not a loadable process image",
            case Drained -> "no more boot handles",
            case QuotaExceeded -> "this process is at its quota",
            case Revoked -> "what this call was waiting for is gone",
            case PeerClosed -> "the other end of this connection is gone",
            // Worded for the condition, not for the op (see the case's
            // docstring): one sentence has to be true of a full ring and of an
            // empty one, because one tag answers both.
            case WouldBlock -> "not right now — try again",
            case Unknown -> "unknown status",
        }
    }

    /// Whether this status reports a failure. The inverse of `Ok`, named so a
    /// caller reads intent rather than a comparison.
    public func is_failure(&self) -> Bool {
        match self {
            case Ok -> false,
            case _ -> true,
        }
    }
}

extension SosStatus: Printable {
    public func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) {
        // `format` has no error channel, and on SOS the builder is fixed
        // storage anyway, which refuses nothing.
        try! into.append(self.describe())
    }
}

extension SosStatus: Error {}

// =============================================================================
// Fault reasons
// =============================================================================

/// Why the kernel terminated a process.
///
/// The other half of the cut `SosStatus` describes: these are the caller errors
/// a process could have checked, and meeting one ends the process rather than
/// returning it a word to branch on. It is the line the language draws at an
/// indexed accessor (an out-of-range index panics, it does not hand back a
/// flag), and it cuts userspace error handling down to the failures that are
/// real.
///
/// A process never receives one of these: it is dead by the time the tag
/// exists. It reaches a human through the kernel's report, and the harness
/// asserts on that line. The first three keep the numbers they had as statuses
/// so a reader of an old transcript and a new one sees the same tag for the
/// same mistake.
///
/// `UInt8` because it is a kernel-internal tag that crosses no register.
public enum FaultReason: UInt8 {
    case BadHandle = 1,
    case BadOp = 2,
    case AccessDenied = 3,
    case NotSelf = 4,
    case BadState = 5,
    case BadEntry = 6,
    case Deadlock = 7,
    case NotWaitable = 8,
    case BadArg = 9,
    case BadAlign = 10,
    case BadBuffer = 11,
    case DuplicateKey = 12,
    case LineBound = 13,
}

extension FaultReason {
    /// The short tag the kernel's termination line carries.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case BadHandle -> "bad handle",
            case BadOp -> "bad op",
            case AccessDenied -> "access denied",
            case NotSelf -> "not the calling thread",
            // The tag covers every object kind, and an Interrupt acked before
            // it fired.
            case BadState -> "object in the wrong state",
            case BadEntry -> "thread entry outside the process",
            case Deadlock -> "every thread blocked",
            case NotWaitable -> "not a waitable",
            case BadArg -> "argument outside its domain",
            case BadAlign -> "buffer is not word-aligned",
            // Direction-neutral: the copy-in and copy-out doors check different
            // windows (outbound must land in the process's writable memory,
            // inbound merely inside the memory it was granted), so a wording
            // naming one of them would be wrong for the other.
            case BadBuffer -> "buffer is outside the process's memory",
            // A give whose tag is already in the child's boot set raises this
            // tag too (an identity naming two handles is broken config,
            // caller-checkable, and would make the record a multimap and the
            // boot lookup ambiguous). The sentence names the Waiter case, but
            // the string is asserted by a shipped transcript (`event_dupkey`),
            // so it is not widened here. Widen it to name the key rather than
            // the attachment when that expectation may change.
            case DuplicateKey -> "an attachment already uses that key",
            case LineBound -> "that interrupt line is already bound",
        }
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Process status (spec §8)
// =============================================================================

/// How a process ended, or that it has not. Spec §8: one fixed-width status
/// word, kind in the high bits and a code in the low bits, so a supervisor
/// reads both out of one register.
///
/// `Killed` is declared and unreachable: there is no op that kills a process.
/// It is here because the word's encoding is what a supervisor parses, and a
/// kind added later would renumber nothing but would still be a wire change.
public enum ProcessStatusKind: UInt8 {
    case Running = 0,
    case Exited = 1,
    case Faulted = 2,
    case Killed = 3,
}

/// Where the kind sits in the status word. The low half is the code (an exit
/// status, or a `FaultReason`), so both fit a 32-bit reader on either profile.
static PROCESS_STATUS_KIND_SHIFT: Int = 16
static PROCESS_STATUS_CODE_MASK: UInt = 0xFFFF

/// Assemble the §8 status word.
public func process_status_word(kind: ProcessStatusKind, code: UInt) -> UInt {
    ((((kind as UInt8) as UInt) << PROCESS_STATUS_KIND_SHIFT)
     | (code & PROCESS_STATUS_CODE_MASK))
}
